350: \(p=P_0u,\ A_1=H_\infty u,\ B_1=H_\infty v\).
351: The rational inspection map with basis \((A_1,B_1)\) sends \(p\) to
352: \((c,d)\), with
353: \[
354:  \begin{array}{c|cc}
355:  &c&d\\ \hline
356:  A&(N-2)/(2N-2)&-a/(2N-2)\\
357:  B&(N-2)/(2N-2)&-a/(2N-2)\\
358:  E&N/(N-1)&bN/(2N-2).
359:  \end{array}                                           \tag{IF.25}
360: \]
361: Every \(c\) is positive for the admitted \(N>2\). At fixed \(r,s>0\)
362: the two top cones give the square \([0,s]^2\), split along its
363: diagonal. The third surviving cone gives
364: \[
365:           (s,0)+[0,r](c,d)+[0,s](0,1).
366: \]
367: Their union is the strip
368: \[
369:  0\le x\le L=s+rc,\qquad f(x)\le y\le f(x)+s,\quad
370:  f(x)=0\ (x\le s),\quad f(x)=(d/c)(x-s)\ (x\ge s).
371: \]
372: The actual period vectors are \((L,rd)\) and \((0,s)\).
373: Reducing \(x\) uniquely modulo \(L\), then reducing
374: \(y-i rd-f(x-iL)\) uniquely modulo \(s\), assigns every point
375: off a boundary to exactly one of these strip cells.
376: The square and parallelogram share their entire vertical edge;
377: the far edge is the next square's near edge; and the upper
378: piecewise linear boundary is the lower one translated by \((0,s)\).
379: Thus all intersections are full common faces. The two collapsed
380: bottom tetrahedra have the already existing face
381: \(\operatorname{cone}(R_0(0),R_0(u),R_1(u+v))\), and the collapsed
382: mixed tetrahedron has
383: \(\operatorname{cone}(R_0(0),R_1(v),R_1(u+v))\).
384: No image of a collapsed cone is omitted from the face complex.
385: This proves the fan and support statements with the exact translations
386: (IF.24), including the rank-one height boundary.
387: 
388: On the H boundary the A and E constructions give the negative
389: unit-square triangulation in \(H_\infty^{-1}\) coordinates; B gives
390: the shear with basis \((e_1,e_2-be_1)\). Their literal polygonal
391: overlay is a common rational periodic refinement. Intersections
392: are common faces because their supporting equalities are those of
393: the two original complexes. It has finitely many cells modulo
394: \(\mathbb Z^2\). All rays are then replaced by their primitive
395: integer vectors on the same ray, preserving their true heights.
396: For \(b=2\) the added H-edge midpoint ray is the primitive multiple
397: of \((2,N-2,0,2)\).
398: 
399: The A boundary retains every primitive ray \((j,0,1,0)\); the B
400: boundary retains \((j,-aj,1,0)\). They are exactly the actual source
401: line chains: on B the literal coordinate \(Y=Z^aV\) has no vanishing
402: line character along that lower face. The E boundary retains all
403: primitive rays \((0,j,1,0)\), not only the raw step-\(N\) rays.
404: Its adjacent coordinates are
405: \[
406:        X_j=V\xi_E^{-j},\quad Y_j=\xi_E^{j+1}/V,\qquad
407:                        X_jY_j=\xi_E .                    \tag{IF.26}
408: \]
409: The actual scalar period shifts \(j\) by \(N\), retaining its
410: nonzero multiplier in the two chart maps.
411: 
412: Extend these boundary refinements by coning already subdivided
413: faces from their primitive ray sum, face by face in increasing
414: dimension. This gives common faces and commutes with every
415: unimodular map (IF.24). The relative regularization then uses a
416: nonzero lattice point in each singular cone's half-open
417: parallelepiped, subdividing all occurrences of its minimal face
418: simultaneously. The incident determinants strictly decrease;
419: the relative separator prevents two translates of that centre
420: in one cone. The protected boundary faces are already regular
421: and hence never contain a resolution centre.
422: 
423: The executable \(\texttt{source\_infinity\_regular\_fan.py}\) is a
424: separate saved adaptation of the earlier complete algorithm.
425: Its derivation file records every changed matrix, rank-one
426: canonical representative, stabilizer and protected-face dimension.
427: It exports every actual primitive ray, determinant-one cone,
428: period, face permutation and higher parameter order. Its legacy
429: tags I,W,E mean the present A,B,E faces; they do not identify A
430: with the earlier full-rank I face.
